## Local Setup

Local setup requires
[uv package manager](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) and `make`.
Run the following commands:

```bash
make install   # venv, dependencies, git hooks
make check     # lint, type checks, tests — same as CI
```

## Development

| Command      | Description |
| ------------ | ----------- |
| `make fmt`   | Format and auto-fix            |
| `make lint`  | Ruff lint + format check       |
| `make type`  | mypy (strict)                  |
| `make test`  | pytest with coverage (min 80%) |
| `make build` | Build wheel into `dist/`       |
{%- if template == 'lambda' %}
| `make e2e E2E_FUNCTION_NAME=...` | E2E tests against a deployed function |
| `make image`      | Build the Lambda image (`linux/arm64`), tagged with the git commit |
| `make image-test` | Build the test image and run the test suite in a container |
{%- endif %}

New dependencies can be added with `uv add <pkg>` (dev tools: `uv add --dev <pkg>`).


## CI/CD

GitHub Actions workflows in `.github/workflows/`:

| Workflow           | Trigger                            | Steps |
| ------------------ | ---------------------------------- | ----- |
| `branch-push.yml`  | push to any branch except `master` | lint, type checks, tests |
{%- if template == 'lambda' %}
| `pull-request.yml` | pull request to `master`           | title prefix check (release or not); lint, type checks, tests; build the image of the head commit and push it to ECR (`{{ ecr_repository }}:e2e-<first 8 characters of the sha>`, skipped if already there); switch `{{ aws_function_name }}-test` to it; E2E tests |
| `master-push.yml`  | push to `master`                   | lint, type checks, tests; with a version prefix: version tag, image `<version>` to ECR, `deploy-test.yml` |
| `deploy-test.yml`  | called by `master-push.yml`, or by hand from a version tag | check the version and its image; deploy test |
| `deploy-prod.yml`  | by hand, from a version tag        | check the tag and its image; deploy prod |
{%- else %}
| `pull-request.yml` | pull request to `master`           | title prefix check (release or not); lint, type checks, tests |
| `master-push.yml`  | push to `master`                   | lint, type checks, tests; with a version prefix: version tag |
{%- endif %}

### Releases

A merge to `master` is a release `major.minor.patch` when the title of the merged pull request
starts with a version prefix; the prefix decides the version (`.github/scripts/next-version.sh`),
from the latest version tag:

| Title starts with | Example           |
| ----------------- | ----------------- |
| `[MAJOR]`         | `1.4.2` → `2.0.0` |
| `[MINOR]`         | `1.4.2` → `1.5.0` |
| `[PATCH]`         | `1.4.2` → `1.4.3` |

The first release is `1.0.0`. A title without a prefix releases nothing (for changes such as README
or workflows). Any other prefix, e.g. `[minor]` or `[WIP]`, fails the pull request's job
"Title Version Prefix", which also reports whether the merge will release; after editing the title,
re-run it. The tag is created on the merge commit once the checks pass, and a re-run of a failed
release keeps its version. `pyproject.toml` keeps the placeholder version `0.0.0`.
{%- if template == 'lambda' %}
The image is `{{ ecr_repository }}:<version>` and its package reports that version (`__version__`).

### Deploys

`deploy-test.yml` deploys every release to test (called by `master-push.yml`). Both deploy
workflows also run by hand: Actions → Deploy test / Deploy prod → Run workflow, and pick the
version tag under "Use workflow from" (a branch is refused). From a terminal:

```bash
gh workflow run deploy-test.yml --ref 1.4.2
gh workflow run deploy-prod.yml --ref 1.4.2
```

An older tag rolls back.

### AWS Prerequisites

The workflows deploy into existing resources (e.g. created by an infrastructure project),
in account `{{ aws_account_id }}`, region `{{ aws_region }}`:

| Resource | Name |
| -------- | ---- |
| ECR repository (immutable tags) | `{{ ecr_repository }}` |
| Lambda functions (container image, `arm64`) | `{{ aws_function_name }}-test`, `{{ aws_function_name }}-prod` |
| IAM roles for GitHub OIDC | `{{ aws_deploy_role_name }}-test`, `{{ aws_deploy_role_name }}-prod` |

The test role pushes images, updates the test function and invokes it (E2E tests);
the prod role updates the prod function. In the repository settings, create the GitHub
environments `test` and `prod`; each role trusts only the jobs of its environment.
